  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 AM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nHigh pressure over Minnesota seeps east through today and is \ncurrently working to disrupt meager lake-effect returns over far \nsoutheast Lake Michigan. A steady does of lake effect clouds this \nmorning are favored to mix out from east to west this morning, \nthrough plenty of high clouds are noted just west of the Mississippi \nRiver. As a result, increasing clouds today which locks in \ntemperatures near 40. \n\nA pair of upper-level troughs are noted on water vapor; one over the \nAlberta-Saskakutchan line and the second digging through the Four \nCorners. These two features track east through time and eventually \nlift northeast late this week. An overall dearth of cold air leaves \nlocal precipitation Wednesday night through Friday to be primary \nrain. However, those with travel interest over central IL should \nnote light snowfall there Wednesday night. This remains a rather odd \nsetup and evolution with two cyclones in close proximity \nabsolutely soaking up Atlantic moisture and spilling it into the \nMidwest. If there are any snowflakes locally, Thursday night may be \nthe \"best\" opportunity as the eastern most surface low occludes and \nnortheasterly fetch through interior Canada promotes somewhat improved \ncold air advection. Overall, a prolonged period of soaking rain \nearly Thursday morning through Friday. Rain will generally total \n0.50 to 0.75\", with lower amounts over Michiana and greater amounts \ntoward Muncie and Lima, OH. \n\nNorthwest flow through the remainder of the weekend may permit \nanother disturbance to pass through, yet run-to-run consistency has \nbeen poor so far. What continues to be advertised is a return to \nzonal flow or perhaps even uper-levle riding to begin December. This \nwould bring us back toward above-normal temperatures.",
